---
import ProductCaseStudyArticle from "../../../../components/ProductCaseStudyArticle.astro";
---

<ProductCaseStudyArticle
  slug="faster-choice-results-pages"
  seoTitle="How to Help Customers Choose Faster on Results Pages | Product Research Case Study"
  dek="A customer on a results page has already told you what they want. Every extra scroll, comparison and second of doubt after that point is friction the product created. This worked example shows how I diagnose it and test the fix."
  summary={[
    "Results pages are often built to show everything. Customers need them to make one decision quickly.",
    "I look at five causes: ranking that ignores intent, hidden navigation, inputs that work against each other, hard price comparison, and conditions shown too late.",
    "Session recordings and click data separate these causes faster than surveys can.",
    "Relevance ranking is a strong first test, but it needs a revenue guardrail so it does not simply push cheaper options.",
    "A sold-out option should become a recommendation, not an error.",
  ]}
  scenario={[
    "Imagine a regional rail operator's booking app. Trains run every 15 to 30 minutes, each with three fares: a flexible fare, a cheaper fare tied to one train, and first class. A search for tomorrow returns more than 60 departures.",
    "The share of customers who move from results to checkout has been flat for a year, and the team suspects the results page.",
  ]}
  hypotheses={[
    {
      name: "Ranking ignores intent.",
      claim: "Results start at the first train of the day, not near the requested time.",
      signal: "Most clicks far down the list; long scrolls before the first click",
      check: "Click position against requested time; scroll depth",
    },
    {
      name: "Navigation is hidden.",
      claim: "Filters exist, but few people find them.",
      signal: "Low filter use alongside long scrolling",
      check: "Filter usage rate; session recordings",
    },
    {
      name: "Inputs conflict.",
      claim: "A time picker in the search form silently excludes options.",
      signal: "Repeated resets of the time field; short or empty result lists",
      check: "Field interaction events; recordings",
    },
    {
      name: "Prices are hard to compare.",
      claim: "Differences are hidden among shared features.",
      signal: "Long time on price selection; switching back and forth",
      check: "Time-to-select distribution; tab switches",
    },
    {
      name: "Conditions come late.",
      claim: "Flexibility rules appear only in checkout.",
      signal: "Hesitation on the cheaper fare; support questions about changes",
      check: "Support tags; review analysis; recordings",
    },
  ]}
  evidence={[
    "Clicks cluster around the requested time, but that train is the 34th item in the list.",
    "Only a small minority of customers open the filters, while recordings show long scrolling and repeated attempts to reach later trains.",
    "One customer resets the time picker to midnight several times while trying to browse another day.",
    "Choosing a fare takes much longer than any other step, and the slowest quarter of customers take over a minute.",
    "When a train sells out between search and selection, most customers search again for the same route and pick a train a little later.",
  ]}
  evidenceNote="On choice: a meta-analysis of choice-overload studies found that the effect of more options depends heavily on context rather than being universal. In practice, that means the fix is better structure, not fewer options."
  sizing={[
    { label: "Results-page sessions a month", value: "100,000" },
    { label: "Sessions that scroll past 20 options", value: "25% = 25,000" },
    { label: "Results-to-checkout: long scrollers vs everyone else", value: "18% vs 30% (12-point gap)" },
    { label: "Share of the gap a fix could close", value: "One quarter = 3 points = 750 checkouts" },
    { label: "Checkout completion × average order", value: "60% × €30" },
  ]}
  sizingResult="About €13,500 a month, if ranking and navigation close a quarter of the gap."
  sizingNote="The gap is a correlation. The test below is what proves how much of it is caused by the page."
  fixes={[
    {
      title: "Rank by relevance",
      pattern: "Clicks cluster near the requested time, but the list starts at the first train.",
      change: "Score options on closeness to the request, duration, changes and price, then add booking behaviour gradually.",
      effect: "The right options appear first.",
    },
    {
      title: "Visible navigation where people look",
      pattern: "Filters exist, but almost nobody uses them.",
      change: "A fixed bar with morning, afternoon and evening shortcuts above the results. Remove the forced time input from the search form.",
      effect: "A two-click path: jump to the time range, then choose.",
    },
    {
      title: "Show a short list first, then load more",
      pattern: "Long lists are slow to load and hard to scan on mobile.",
      change: "Show the best matches first, let customers expand the full list, and load more as they scroll.",
      effect: "Faster first render and less choice overload.",
    },
    {
      title: "Compare prices vertically",
      pattern: "Side-by-side fares hide the differences.",
      change: "Stack the options, show shared features once, and highlight only what differs: flexibility, refunds, seat.",
      effect: "Faster, more confident choice.",
    },
    {
      title: "Show conditions on the card",
      pattern: "Customers worry about what happens if their plans change.",
      change: "Show \"valid on later trains today\" or \"this train only\" on the card, with a distinct icon for each.",
      effect: "Less hesitation and fewer wrong choices.",
    },
    {
      title: "Turn sold-out options into a recommendation",
      pattern: "After a sold-out error, most customers retry the same route a little later.",
      change: "Keep the original choice visible and offer the next available option with its time and price difference.",
      effect: "A recoverable failure instead of a fresh search.",
    },
    {
      title: "Honest urgency, never invented",
      pattern: "Same-day and advance customers behave differently.",
      change: "Show real, inventory-backed availability to urgent customers and early-booking benefits to planners.",
      effect: "Relevant reassurance for both groups, without damaging trust.",
    },
  ]}
  priorities={[
    { cells: ["Visible navigation", "High", "High", "Low", "1"] },
    { cells: ["Sold-out recommendation", "Medium", "High", "Low", "2"] },
    { cells: ["Relevance ranking", "High", "Medium", "Medium", "3"] },
    { cells: ["Vertical price comparison", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium", "4"] },
    { cells: ["Conditions on cards, short list, urgency", "Medium", "Medium", "Low–Medium", "Next"] },
  ]}
  prioritiesNote="Cheap, high-confidence fixes go first. Ranking has the largest upside but needs a proper test, so it follows once tracking is in place."
  test={[
    { label: "Hypothesis", text: "Ranking options around the requested time increases results-to-checkout without lowering revenue per visitor." },
    { label: "Primary metric", text: "Results-to-checkout rate." },
    { label: "Guardrails", text: "Revenue per visitor and average order value, because a ranking can win on conversion by pushing cheaper options." },
    { label: "Supporting metrics", text: "Time to first click, click position, scroll depth, and price-selection time (median and 75th percentile)." },
    { label: "Segments", text: "Same-day vs advance, mobile vs desktop. A change can help one group and hurt another." },
  ]}
  elsewhere={[
    "Any product that returns a long list has the same problem. An e-commerce category with 400 products, a marketplace with 80 providers, a SaaS pricing page with four plans and twenty rows of features: the customer still has to make one choice, quickly and with confidence.",
    "The levers are the same: rank by what the customer asked for, put navigation where people look, compare by differences, and show conditions before the decision rather than after it.",
  ]}
  checklist={[
    "Search for a typical request on a phone. How many swipes until the best answer is on screen?",
    "Check your filter usage rate. If it is in single digits, the filters are not part of the experience.",
    "Time yourself choosing between options. Can you name the difference in one sentence?",
    "Trigger a sold-out or unavailable error. Does the page offer the next best option?",
    "Find where conditions such as flexibility or refunds are first shown. If it is in checkout, it is too late.",
  ]}
  conclusion="A results page should make a decision easy, not just make every option available. Ranking around intent, putting navigation where people look and comparing options by their differences shortens the path to a confident purchase. Each change has a clear behavioural metric, which makes it a good candidate for an A/B test."
  contactLine="Do customers scroll, compare and still leave? I can help you find where the decision gets stuck and test the fix."
  sources={[
    {
      name: "Scheibehenne, Greifeneder & Todd (2010) — Can there ever be too many options? A meta-analytic review of choice overload",
      url: "https://archive-ouverte.unige.ch/unige:76440",
    },
    {
      name: "Kellogg Insight — When are consumers most likely to feel overwhelmed by their options?",
      url: "https://insight.kellogg.northwestern.edu/article/what-predicts-consumer-choice-overload",
    },
  ]}
/>
